\uline{Corollaries} ``purely geometric'':

\uline{Corollary} 1 Let $X,Y$ be \inserted{reduced connected finite-type} schemes over an algebraically closed field, $k$, and $f,g:X\longrightarrow Y$ two morphisms, we assume that $\pi_1(f),\pi_1(g):\pi_1(X)\rightrightarrows\pi_1(Y)$ are equal (as outer homomorphisms). Then

a) If $Y$ \deleted{is \unsure{an|an} extension \unsure{of an|of an}} \inserted{embeds via an $i:Y\longrightarrow G$ into a commutative algebraic group \unsure{$G$}} extension of an abelian variety by a torus, there exists an $a\in Y$ (unique) such that $g(x)=f(x)+a$ $\forall x\in X(k)$, that is, $(i\circ g)=\tau_a\circ(i\circ f)$ ($\tau_a$ the translation by $a$).

b) If $Y$ is an elementary variety of \unsure{Artin} or \deleted{\unsure{a}} \unsure{successively fibred in} elementary \unsure{varieties}, \inserted{and $X$ \deleted{normal} \inserted{irreducible} and if $f$ or $g$ is dominant,} then $f=g$.

\margin{\uuline{b)} \deleted{This case is \unsure{proved} \unsure{by us} in characteristic $0$ \unsure{for curves}; in characteristic $p>0$, we know \unsure{nothing about the} structure of $\pi_1$ (\unsure{pro-$p$}, \unsure{pro-prime-to-$p$}), \unsure{and even} the centres of the $\pi_1$\ldots}}
\margin{\unsure{A strong conjecture would establish the uniqueness of} \unsure{morphisms}, with $X$ irreducible and \unsure{whatever the} \unsure{maps} $f,g$, \unsure{without assuming them|must assume them} \unsure{dominant}.}
\margin{In fact, in a) it suffices to assume that $H_1(f,\mathbb Z_\ell)=H_1(g,\mathbb Z_\ell)$ for an $\ell$ prime to the characteristic \deleted{\unsure{exponent}}}

\uline{Proof} a) The uniqueness of $a$ is clear -- and \deleted{\unsure{it}} \inserted{existence} amounts, \inserted{replacing $Y$ by $G$ and} on choosing an ``origin'' $a\in X(k)$, and on reducing to the case $f(a)=g(a)=0$, to proving that then $f=g$. Moreover the hypothesis $\pi_1(f)=\pi_1(g)$ can be weakened to the hypothesis that \deleted{$f$ is} $H_1(f,\mathbb Z_\ell)=H_1(g,\mathbb Z_\ell)$, \inserted{$\ell$ prime to the characteristic,} for here it suffices
\margin{23}
